      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{q+1}-2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{q+1}+2.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{q+1}+2,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{q+1}-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{q+1}-2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{q+1}+2}{\\sqrt{q+1}+2} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(\\sqrt{q+1}+2)}{(\\sqrt{q+1}-2)(\\sqrt{q+1}+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(\\sqrt{q+1}+2)}{(\\sqrt{q+1})^{2}-{2}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(\\sqrt{q+1}+2)}{q+1-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(\\sqrt{q+1}+2)}{q-3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
