      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{w+1}{\\sqrt{2w+3}-1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{2w+3}+1.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{2w+3}+1,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{w+1}{\\sqrt{2w+3}-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{w+1}{\\sqrt{2w+3}-1}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2w+3}+1}{\\sqrt{2w+3}+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{(w+1)(\\sqrt{2w+3}+1)}{(\\sqrt{2w+3}-1)(\\sqrt{2w+3}+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{(w+1)(\\sqrt{2w+3}+1)}{(\\sqrt{2w+3})^{2}-{1}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{(w+1)(\\sqrt{2w+3}+1)}{2w+3-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{(w+1)(\\sqrt{2w+3}+1)}{2w+2} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(w+1)}(\\sqrt{2w+3}+1)}{2\\cancel{(w+1)}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2w+3}+1}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
