      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{x+3}-\\sqrt{x+5}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{x+3}-\\sqrt{x+5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{x+3}-\\sqrt{x+5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5}}{\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{12(\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5})}{(\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5})(\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5})} & = \\\\ \\frac{12(\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5})}{(\\sqrt{x+3})^{2}-(\\sqrt{x+5})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{12(\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5})}{(x+3)-(x+5)} & = \\\\ \\frac{12(\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5})}{-2} & = \\\\ -6(\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
